"佐用町",0,0],["三日月町",0,2005],["上月町",0,2005],["南光町",0,2005]],"28585":[["村岡町",0,2005],["美方町",0,2005],["香住町",0,2005]],"28586":[["浜坂町",0,2005],["温泉町",0,2005]]},"yurai":{"28":{"神戸市":"The houses around today's Sannomiya were kanbe, households supporting Ikuta Shrine; \"kanbe\" became \"kōbe\".","姫路市":"From Himeji Hill, the old name of the castle hill, already in the Harima Fudoki; a link to silkworms (himeko) is also suggested.","尼崎市":"\"Ama\" (people who made their living from the sea) plus \"saki\" (a point of land); it first meant only the shore south of today's Daimotsu Station. The late-Edo Morisada Mankō already records the short form \"Ama\".","宝塚市":"\"Zuka\" means a burial mound; several mounds have been called Takarazuka and none is certain. A 1672 land survey already lists hamlets such as Takarazuka-higashi; the name passed to a spa and a station in Meiji and became a municipality name only in 1951.","伊丹市":"Several theories: the inlet of the ancient \"Itoumi\" sea, a site on the flat \"board\" of a river terrace, or a word for crumbling riverbanks.","三田市":"Long said to come from the \"three fields of merit\" written inside the Maitreya statue of Konshin-ji, but the new city history could not confirm it, and it is now treated as legend. \"Sanda Village\" first appears in a document of 1477.","三木市":"Legend says that when Empress Jingū rested here, locals offered sake (miki) in a fine jar (mitsubo) — giving Miki, and the district name Minō."}},"kyu":{"28101":{"t":19,"k":4988,"r":[["兵庫県",3144],["Amagasaki Domain",1844]]},"28102":{"t":20,"k":4432,"r":[["兵庫県",2702],["Amagasaki Domain",1488],["@hatamoto",207],["Koga Domain",20],["@other",15]]},"28105":{"t":9,"k":5942,"r":[["兵庫県",5246],["@hatamoto",632],["@jisha",64]]},"28106":{"t":5,"k":2958,"r":[["@hatamoto",1106],["兵庫県",1000],["Koga Domain",853]]},"28107":{"t":10,"k":4727,"r":[["兵庫県",4322],["Asao Domain",377],["@jisha",28]]},"28108":{"t":10,"k":4028,"r":[["Akashi Domain",3986],["@jisha",42]]},"28109":{"t":63,"k":24267,